Inside a `'use cache'` function you call `cacheLife(profile)` where profile can be `'seconds'`, `'minutes'`, `'hours'`, `'days'`, `'weeks'`, `'max'` — each maps to `{ stale, revalidate, expire }` defaults (e.g., `'hours'` is `stale 300, revalidate 3600, expire 86400`).

For product-specific freshness, define custom profiles in `next.config.


| Profile | stale | revalidate | expire | Use |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `seconds` | 0 | 1 | — | realtime pricing |
| `minutes` | 30 | 60 | 300 | cart |
| `hours` | 300 | 3600 | 86400 | blog CMS |
| `days` | 300 | 86400 | 604800 | marketing |
| custom `blog` | 60 | 600 | 3600 | your config |

**Interview Tip:** Show custom `experimental.cacheLife` in next.config and tag granularity — interviewers test if you know `expire` vs `revalidate`.

```js
// next.config.ts — custom cacheLife profiles
const nextConfig = {
  experimental: {
    cacheLife: {
      blog: { stale: 60, revalidate: 600, expire: 3600 },
      product: { stale: 10, revalidate: 30, expire: 300 }
    }
  }
};
export default nextConfig;
